### What is the difference between map and flatMap?

### Visual comparison

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class MapVsFlatMap {
    record Customer(String name, List<String> accountIds) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Customer> customers = List.of(
            new Customer("Rajesh", List.of("SAV2001", "CUR3001")),
            new Customer("Priya", List.of("SAV2002")),
            new Customer("Amit", List.of("SAV2003", "FD4001", "CUR3002"))
        );

        // map: Customer -> List<String> (NESTED!)
        List<List<String>> nested = customers.stream()
            .map(Customer::accountIds)
            .toList();
        System.out.println("map: " + nested);
        // [[SAV2001, CUR3001], [SAV2002], [SAV2003, FD4001, CUR3002]]

        // flatMap: Customer -> Stream<String> -> FLAT!
        List<String> flat = customers.stream()
            .flatMap(c -> c.accountIds().stream())
            .toList();
        System.out.println("flatMap: " + flat);
        // [SAV2001, CUR3001, SAV2002, SAV2003, FD4001, CUR3002]
    }
}
```

```text
map: [[SAV2001, CUR3001], [SAV2002], [SAV2003, FD4001, CUR3002]]
flatMap: [SAV2001, CUR3001, SAV2002, SAV2003, FD4001, CUR3002]
```

### Comparison table

| Aspect | map | flatMap |
|--------|-----|--------|
| Mapping | T -> R | T -> Stream<R> |
| Cardinality | 1-to-1 | 1-to-many |
| Result | Stream<R> (may be nested) | Stream<R> (always flat) |
| Use case | Transform type | Flatten nested structures |

### flatMap with Optional (Java 9)

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class FlatMapOptional {
    record Account(String id, Optional<String> email) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", Optional.of("rajesh@bank.com")),
            new Account("SAV2002", Optional.empty()),
            new Account("CUR3001", Optional.of("amit@bank.com"))
        );

        // flatMap(Optional::stream) extracts present values, removes empties
        List<String> emails = accounts.stream()
            .map(Account::email)
            .flatMap(Optional::stream)
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Emails: " + emails);
    }
}
```

```text
Emails: [rajesh@bank.com, amit@bank.com]
```

### flatMap with String splitting

```java
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

List<String> allSymbols = List.of("RELIANCE,TCS,HDFC", "INFY,WIPRO").stream()
    .flatMap(csv -> Arrays.stream(csv.split(",")))
    .distinct()
    .toList();
// [RELIANCE, TCS, HDFC, INFY, WIPRO]
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

flatMap is the monadic bind operation. It parallels Optional.flatMap exactly: if a function returns an Optional, use flatMap to avoid nested Optional<Optional<T>>. In streams, if a function returns a Stream, use flatMap to avoid nested Stream<Stream<T>>. The rule: if you see a nested container, flatMap unwraps it.
